From: Oever Gonzalez <software@notengobattery.com>
Subject: [PATCH] mt76: npu: ring the rx doorbell after refilling the queue

The NPU rx rings expose the usual Airoha register quad -- desc_base,
ring_size, cpu_idx, dma_idx -- at REG_RX_BASE(qid), and CPU_IDX is how
the host announces how far it has armed descriptors. airoha_eth drives
the identical block and ends every refill with it:

  airoha_qdma_fill_rx_queue()
      if (nframes)
              airoha_qdma_rmw(qdma, REG_RX_CPU_IDX(qid), ...,
                              FIELD_PREP(RX_RING_CPU_IDX_MASK, q->head));

mt76_npu_fill_rx_queue() writes no register at all. It re-arms slots and
advances its own q->head, and the NPU is never told. The only index the
driver publishes is DMA_IDX -- the engine's own position -- written from
mt76_npu_dequeue(), which is exactly the path that stops running once
the ring stalls.

So the two sides have no way back once they disagree: the driver reports
every slot armed while the NPU reports its host adapter queue
permanently full, and nothing short of the probe-time desc_base write
restores a common view. On a Gemtek W1700K this deadlocks the whole
NPU->WLAN handoff, silently and permanently, whenever a burst arrives at
the transition where hardware flow offload is removed under load.

Announce q->head on CPU_IDX after a refill, matching airoha_eth. The
register's upper half carries nothing, so a plain write is fine.

--- a/npu.c
+++ b/npu.c
@@ -47,6 +47,12 @@
 		nframes++;
 	}
 
+	/* the NPU tracks free slots through CPU_IDX; without this it never
+	 * learns about refilled descriptors and stalls with its queue full
+	 */
+	if (nframes)
+		Q_WRITE(q, cpu_idx, q->head);
+
 	return nframes;
 }
 
